From 14cd1ff45744f699a993433875e05dcdcf99c62c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 05:48:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: play the video without sound while the audio
 engine has no sink

When the audio engine cannot give the audio player an output,
CAudioSinkAE::AddPackets() takes nothing, CVideoPlayerAudio offers the
same frame again and again and reads no more packets, its queue fills
up, CVideoPlayer::Process() stops reading the demuxer while a queue is
full, and the video starves a few seconds into the file with the clock
running: a black (or frozen) picture under a time bar that advances.
Seen on a machine with two GPUs whose audio device was an HDMI output of
the GPU without the TV: Dolby Vision playback on the other one showed
"Dolby Vision" on the TV and no picture.

Until an output has taken data of the stream, the audio player now tries
the output again twice a second, and:

- once the engine has said for a second and a half that it has no sink
  (IAE::HasNoSink()), it drops each frame when its time comes instead of
  holding it, so the video plays and the queue keeps its level. A stream
  that the engine accepted before it failed to open the sink for it is
  freed. After five seconds, the player is told
  (PLAYER_AUDIO_OUTPUT_FAILED): it closes the audio stream, plays the
  video alone and tells the user once; a file with nothing but audio
  ends as one that cannot be played;
- otherwise (the engine is suspended while the display changes its mode,
  busy opening a slow sink, or without its sink for an instant) the
  frame waits for the output, as before. Before, an output that could
  not be created in that state was never tried again.

After the stream was closed, no audio stream is opened by itself until
the next file or until the user chooses one, with one exception. If the
engine never accepted the stream (it had no sink at all) and gets a sink
later (the TV or the receiver was switched on), the stream that was
closed is chosen again as the user would do it, at most three times for
each file. If the engine had accepted the stream, it is the stream that
it cannot play, and nothing is tried by itself.

While the engine says for the first second and a half that it has no
sink, the frame waits and the audio player does not report that it has
started: a file that starts while the sink is away for a moment keeps
all its sound.

An output that has taken data is left to the engine as before: if it
stops later (a TV switched off during the film), the player waits for
it.

The text is a core string (English, 39996) with its Spanish translations
in Estuary (31692), as the other Hades texts.
